sentences.l:
As when smoke, going from a town, may reach the upper air
from afar, from an island, which enemies fight around,
who all day long are decided by hateful Ares
out of their own town;

And the charioteers were astounded, when they saw an untiring fire,
dread, above the head of the great-hearted son of Peleus,
blazing;

But the Achaeans,
gladly Patroclus from under the missiles having dragged out,
laid him down on a bed;

for I bid, I at least,
to the town now to go, not to wait for heavenly dawn
on the plain by the ships;

but if he will find us being here
tomorrow, having set out with his armour, well indeed someone
will come to know him;

for gladly will he come to sacred Ilium
whoever may flee, and many dogs and vultures will eat
of the Trojans;

But now, when indeed to me the son of Cronus of crooked cunning has given
glory to win at the ships, and to hem the Achaeans against the sea,
foolish one, no longer show these thoughts among the people;
